## 7. Slice plan

Each slice is independently shippable and is **one pull request**. A slice that
cannot state its own ship gate is two slices or one unfinished thought.

| Slice | Story | Scope | Gate to ship |
|---|---|---|---|
| `:1` | `ST-<NNN>` | <smallest useful substrate, with its tests> | <gates green> |
| `:2` | `ST-<NNN>` | <wire into the live path, behind a flag> | <flag default off until the probe reports> |
| `:3` | `ST-<NNN>` | <surface, docs, the doc-map rows> | <the surfaces in DOCS_MAP moved> |

Dependencies between slices are stated here, not implied by the order:
`<ST_NNN>` needs `<ST_NNN>`'s interface.

Two laws bind the plan's shape:

- **Every producer's consumer is in the plan.** A slice that adds a producer
  without the thing that reads it is half a slice, and says so - a computed
  signal nothing consumes is decoration, not delivery (WORKFLOW section 5's
  consumer line, applied at planning time where it is cheapest).
- **Skeleton before depth, at the program level.** Where this arc is part of
  standing up a system: every subsystem present, wired and exercisable - 
  honest stubs included - before any one is deep; then deepen one vertical
  slice at a time, each shippable, the system continuously runnable. No
  "refactoring for two months" period; integration risk is paid down while
  every architectural mistake is still cheap to rework. The discipline is
  resisting the urge to deepen one slice early.

